iable  qualities during his time studying law  at the University of Freeburg in Germany  this was the mid-1930s and Nazism was on  the rise  whilst many kept their opinions on the  newly installed fascist government to  themselves  Popov wrote a series of newspaper  articles that openly ridiculed Hitler  and his cronies it's therefore  unsurprising that upon completing his  PhD in 1937 he was arrested by the  Gestapo the Nazis much feared secret  police and thrown in jail luckily  popov's family connections saw to it  that he was only in The Slammer for  eight days his father managed to get the  Yugoslavian prime minister himself to  personally intervene and secure his  son's release  Popoff then moved to Dubrovnik in  Croatia and spent the next few years  practicing law and chasing women  something he needed no practice at  whatsoever  it wasn't all law school and ladies  though there was a small matter of the  second world war starting up and  although the German invasion of  Yugoslavia didn't take place until April  1941. Popov became involved with the war  effort in 1940. through a friend named  Johann Johnny deppson like Popov yepsen  came from a wealthy family he was a son  of a German shipping magnate and the two  had gotten to know each other at  Freeburg University  James bonding not over liking the same  brand of supernoodles as most students  do but because of a shared love of  sports cars but there was more to yebson  than met the eye because it turned out  he'd been recruited by the adver the  German military intelligence service not  that he was a big Nazi sympathizer being  part of the advert simply meant he could  still travel as much as he needed for  his business  on the Proviso that he sent back  information when requested  to begin with Popov wasn't overly  impressed with his friend's new role  passing information to the Nazis but he  soon came to realize that working for  German intelligence was the perfect  opportunity to work against the regime  and so when yebson suggested that Popov  should also become an advert agent he  agreed immediately rather than celebrate  the new job Popov went straight to the  British Embassy in Belgrade to tell them  what had happened and they referred him  to their passport control office which  was in fact a cover for the MI6 agents  who were working there  amongst them Worcester wonderfully named  Clement hope and it was he who enrolled  Popov as a double agent the  International import export business in  which popov's family were involved  proved excellent cover for both his  so-called work for the Germans and his  real work for the British he relocated  to London but made frequent trips to  Lisbon where the advert handlers were  based Portugal played an important role  in the war as it remained officially  neutral throughout the conflict  that along with its key position on the  Atlantic Ocean and as a point of entry  into Spain made it a magnet for spies  from both sides during his time in  Lisbon pop Huff would provide the  Germans with either unimportant or  simply downright false information given  to him by the British and it was here in  August 1941 that he crossed paths with  Ian Fleming at the time Fleming was  working as an assistant to rear Admiral  John Godfrey the director of Naval  intelligence incidentally it's rumored  that Godfrey served as the inspiration  for Fleming's character M the fictional  leader of MI6 they were returning from a  visit to Washington with a stopover in  Lisbon and whilst Godfrey continued back  to London Fleming remained in the  Portuguese capital for several weeks  presumably taking the opportunity to  sample some of those delicious custard  tart thingies whilst also overseeing an  active International Espionage on  you see whilst Fleming was there Popoff  was undertaking an operation codenamed  Midas to steal forty thousand dollars  from adver high up major ludovico Von  karstoff the advert were happy to give  pop off the cash as they believed he was  busy setting up a network of spies in  England and he was using those funds to  pay them in reality he was simply  passing the loot straight to the Allies  Admiral Godfrey had ordered Fleming to  remain in Lisbon to ensure that once  Popoff had the cash he did The Honorable  thing and gave it to the people he'd  actually stolen it for it was no secret  that Popoff was an avid Gambler and with  thirty thousand dollars burning a hole  in his pocket that's about 600 Grand in  today's money the temptation of having a  little flutter must have been enormous  so enormous in fact that after enjoying  a slap up meal in a nice restaurant  pop-off did in fact make a beeline for  the nearest casino with of a nervous in  Fleming following close behind Fleming  was right to be nervous too because  accompanied by gasps from other gamblers  pop off promptly placed the entire sum  in his possession on a single hand of  baccarat to call the bluff of another  Gambler who'd arrogantly insisted on  playing no limits  as it happens the other Gambler was  indeed bluffing because he quickly  backed down and the sun was never  actually wagered but it seems it's  absolutely ball a move planted a seed in  Fleming's mind because a remarkably  similar scene appears in Fleming's debut  Bond novel Casino Royale where our hero  uses MI6 funds to humiliate Soviet agent  Le Schiff taking 80 million francs from  him in a tense game of baccarat popov's  war effort went far beyond funneling  funds to the allies and inspiring future  literary icons directly after making a  big impression in Portugal he was sent  by the advert to America where they  hoped he could set up another aspiring  similar to the one he'd pretended to set  up in the UK  there was also a request from Germany's  new ally Japan who suspiciously wanted  as much information as Popoff could  obtain about the American Naval Base at  Pearl Harbor in Honolulu Hawaii when  Popoff arrived in the U.S he went to the  FBI to explain why he'd been sent and  raised the alarm about a possible attack  on a key military Target  but he struggled to gain an audience  with FBI director J Edgar Hoover who  rumor has it wasn't the biggest fan of  British spies operating in America  Hoover did eventually come down from  Washington to meet Popov but the whole  thing could hardly have gone any worse  as Hoover seemed personally affronted by  popov's decadent Playboy lifestyle it  probably didn't help that Popoff had  rocked up with a glamorous young British  woman and Hoover threatens to have this  by arrested under the man act which  forbade men to transport women across  state lines for any moral purpose as it  happened Popoff wasn't arrested and  however moral or otherwise that  relationship with his traveling  companion might have been it didn't last  long because he soon broke it off to  begin an affair with friends film star  Simone Simon who was in the U.S filming  horror classic cat people nothing like  keeping a low profile when you're a spy  hey  flings with movie stars aside popov's  trip to America wasn't exactly a great  success the advert were annoyed that he  hadn't been given permission to travel  to Hawaii and the British were miffed  that one of their star agents had been  largely ignored  the information pop-off passed to the  FBI was for whatever reason not acted  upon and so on the 7th of December 1941  the Japanese Air Force launched a  devastating attack on Pearl Harbor  whilst in America popov's lifestyle  remained as lavish as ever and British  documents Declassified in 2002 so that  he ran up huge bills spending money on a  luxury car a penthouse on Park Avenue in  New York and skiing holidays in the  mountains of Idaho  what popov's value as an agent was  undeniable and he was soon to make his  greatest contribution yet to the Allies  winning the war as he played a crucial  role in Operation Fortitude the lengthy  campaign of disinformation designed to  conceal the date and location of the  main Allied invasion of France The  Landing was planned to take place at  Normandy but after a series of fake  operations and false information leaks  by agents like Popov Hitler became  convinced the Allies would come straight  over the English Channel from Dover and  attempt to land in Calais Operation  Fortitude was a huge success leaving the  German forces suitably scattered when  the D-Day Landings took place on the 6th  of June 1944.  it proved to be a turning point in the  war with victory in Europe coming less  than a year later amongst a celebration  however for Popoff there was also  personal sadness in the weeks prior to  the Normandy invasion his great friend  Johnny yepsen was exposed as a double  agent it turned out popoff's old friend  had been working against the Nazis all  along when yebson's deception was  discovered he was arrested by the  gestapo and tortured for information  remarkably he didn't break even under  interrogation and he never released the  names of his accomplices including Popov  in the end the Epson was taken to a  concentration camp and tragically  he was never seen again after the war  Popoff was sent to Paris to try and  establish a British intelligence Network  whilst he was there the so-called Iron  Curtain fell across Europe and  Yugoslavia became a communist country  this strictly no fun ideology was never  going to appeal to someone who used to  charge the cost of his silk shirts to  his spymaster employers so Popoff stayed  in the west he got married had some  children got divorced got married to a  second wife he was almost 30 years  younger than him had some more children  and lived in the Hills behind cons in  the south of France textbooks still for  a retired secret agent not that anybody  actually knew about his former life by  the way he didn't reveal his past to  anyone not even friends or family  but in 1972 Sir John masterman who'd  been responsible for controlling the  Allies double agents during the war  wrote all about it in a book called the  double cross system as one of the Allies  star agents popov's name naturally  featured quite a bit and with the cat  well and truly out of the bag Popoff  himself followed suit and in 1974  released his own book entitled spy  counter spy which was a somewhat  embellished autobiography making the  most of his bondian lifestyle Popov  lived another seven years continuing his  heavy smoking and hard drinking ways  just as James Bond the character he  inspired and Ian Fleming the author who  created him had also both done  he died age 69 in 1981. same year that  for your eyes only the 12th official  Bond movie was released by this point  the most famous spy in screen history  had been pulling in the crowds for  almost two decades and nobody did it  better  although the man on whom he is based may  be much less well known his place in  history is also secure after he  dedicated five years of his life to  fighting the Nazis with bravery and  Ingenuity and what a life it was pop-off  traveled the world dated numerous  beautiful women eight at the finest  restaurants and gambled with Millions he  risked his life for the greater good and  had a bloody good time whilst doing it  all in all for a man codenamed tricycle  it was quite a ride  thanks for watching
 

